WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.560
MATTHEW SOLDO: Hey, everybody.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.460
Welcome to my talk about
buildpacks on Google Cloud.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:11.810
My name is Matt Soldo.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:15.860
I am a product manager at Google
focusing on our serverless

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.100
compute platforms, Cloud
Run, App Engine, and Cloud

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.090
Functions.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:21.560
And for the past
few months, I've

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.860
been working on bringing Google
Cloud's buildpacks to market.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.190
And I'm really excited about
talking to you about them

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:29.990
today.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:32.369
So what we'll be covering
in this talk-- first of all,

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.700
we'll go over what
buildpacks are.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.340
We'll talk about why you
might want to use buildpacks.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.210
And finally, we'll look
at buildpack support

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.330
on Google Cloud, how to use
buildpacks on Google Cloud,

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:47.360
and we'll do a nice demo tying
a few of our products together

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.760
using buildpacks.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.940
So just in case you don't
want to sit through the entire

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.740
presentation, let's do a tl;dr.
So buildpacks create containers

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.780
without using a dockerfile.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.450
You just give the
buildpack your source code,

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.090
and the buildpack detects
what language you're using

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:08.090
and creates a runnable container
that you can run locally

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.030
with Docker, or we could run
on the Kubernetes platform,

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.930
or anywhere else.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.050
So let's just take a
look at that in action

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:16.805
on my laptop right now.

00:01:16.805 --> 00:01:19.700
OK, so to use buildpacks,
we used the pack command.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.400
That is the reference
implementation

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.490
of a buildpack platform.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:29.342
And you can see the available
commands on pack here.

00:01:29.342 --> 00:01:31.550
And we're just going to use
the most basic one, which

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.320
is pack build.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.160
By default, it builds
the current directory.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.920
And we're in the--

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.860
you can see-- this
java-maven directory here.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:47.420
And you just need to provide
the name of the container image

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.920
that you're creating.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.260
And we'll call this
hello-buildpacker.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.160
And so you can see it went
through a detection phase

00:01:55.160 --> 00:02:00.350
where it found a few buildpacks,
a Java runtime, Java-Maven and

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.150
Java entry point.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:03.830
It did some analysis.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:09.259
And it is now building
this application.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:13.460
You can see it's using the
Google Java runtime buildpack

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.330
and using Java 11.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.360
That's specified by our
definition, the Google

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.280
buildpacks definition, of what
to do when it finds a Java app.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.470
And it is now
finishing up its build.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.380
And it has successfully
done and created

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.040
this hello-buildpacker image.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.880
So now I can just run that.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:41.510
And we'll bind it
to port 80 so we

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.040
can load it in our web browser.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:49.310
This is a server that
I have up in GCE.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:52.319
And that was hello-buildpacker.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.120
And it runs.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.450
And there we go.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.370
It's started.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:03.235
And now we go over
to our web browser.

00:03:03.235 --> 00:03:04.610
This is the address
of my server.

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:05.750
It wasn't working before.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.250
But if I do a reload--

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.710
boom, hello,
buildpacker-- it works.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:12.570
And so you can see, in
just a couple of seconds,

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:19.190
we built a Java app and got it
running in a Docker container.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:24.580
And that is a quick
demo of buidlpacks.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.540
So that was
buildpacks in action.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.580
Now buildpacks allow
you to take advantage

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:30.530
of the benefits of
containers without needing

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:31.640
to understand them.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.410
So you can isolate your code.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.600
You can create
microservices architectures.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.647
You can run on orchestration
platforms like Kubernetes

00:03:38.647 --> 00:03:40.730
without needing to write
dockerfiles or understand

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.020
the inner workings of Docker--

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.620
so super cool.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.980
Now recently, Google
Cloud released a set

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.450
of open source buildpacks.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:49.220
You can find them on
GitHub at the address

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.360
that you see in this screenshot,
googlecloudplatform/buildpacks.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.450
And these cover a bunch of
the most popular languages--

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:02.090
Go, JavaScript, Java, PHP,
Python, .NET, and Ruby.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:06.830
And they are designed to create
secure container images, which

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:12.410
can run on any of the runtimes
from Google Cloud such as Cloud

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.248
Run, GKE, or Anthos.

00:04:14.248 --> 00:04:16.040
And of course, because
they're open source,

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.230
you're able to fork them and
use them however you'd like.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.320
Now even though
they're relatively new,

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.740
these buildpacks have powered
over 10 million builds

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:25.860
in production.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.960
And that's because they started
off as an implementation detail

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.560
for App Engine, and
Google Cloud Functions.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:38.630
And they've been powering all of
the source builds on those two

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.790
products for a while now.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:44.600
So we're pretty confident
in their robustness.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:48.950
And you can use them
confidently too in production.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.860
But in addition to App
Engine Cloud Functions,

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.650
Google Cloud buildpacks
are available on many

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:55.880
of our products.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.580
There's support for them in
Cloud Build, Cloud Run, Cloud

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.550
Shell, Cloud Code and Skaffold.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:05.100
And we'll dive into the
specifics of that later on.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.680
So that was the overview.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.090
Let's dive in.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:10.490
What are buildpacks, exactly?

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.500
They are a CNCF sandbox project.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.790
Those are newer projects in
the cloud-native project.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:19.305
And you could find
them at buildpacks.io.

00:05:19.305 --> 00:05:21.200
There are several
large companies

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.830
contributing to the project
in addition to Google.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:29.620
Pivotal Cloud Foundry is a
major backer, as is Salesforce,

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:31.153
via the Heroku division.

00:05:31.153 --> 00:05:33.320
And like we said before,
buildpacks take your source

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.597
code, and they
create a container.

00:05:35.597 --> 00:05:37.430
But there's a little
more to them than this.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.920
And let's take a look at that.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.200
Buildpacks actually
run in a container

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.310
themselves called a builder.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.420
So here you can see, in
a little more detail,

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:52.910
that a container called the
builder runs the buildpacks.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.790
And you can see there's actually
two scripts that they run.

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:57.380
The first one is
a detect script.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:00.350
And that detects what
language your code is.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:05.430
And the detection, it's
actually not rocket science.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:08.840
It's an ordered list of tests.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.600
And the first test that
comes back positive

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.290
is declared the language.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.320
So these tests can
be very simple, like,

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:17.540
is there a packages.json?

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:21.320
And if that's true, then it's
a JavaScript application.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.750
And you can, of
course, override those.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.510
But you'll find that they're--
they usually work just fine.

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:28.700
And then after the
detection script happens,

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:33.560
a build step happens that
actually does the building.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:37.150
So the builder, it's,
itself, an OCI image.

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:39.340
It contains all of the
buildpacks, and groups,

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:41.560
and binaries needed
to do the building.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:43.785
And it runs on a platform.

00:06:43.785 --> 00:06:45.910
And that could be as simple
as the pack CLI command

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:48.940
that you saw in the demo, or
something like Cloud Build

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:50.080
or Tekton.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.560
And that actually
runs the image.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.310
And you can see here
a little CLI snapshot.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:00.310
The pack command, if you
invoke suggest builders,

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:03.710
it'll show you some of the
builders that are known to it.

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:06.190
And Google is one of those.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:07.750
This is also showing
Paketo, which

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:10.743
are the-- that's the branding
of the buildpakcs from Pivotal

00:07:10.743 --> 00:07:11.910
that were released recently.

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:15.050
They're also very good.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:18.280
And just to add
another layer, there's

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.500
also a thing called
a buildpack group.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:23.370
buildpacks themselves are
very small units of execution.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:25.120
They can be designed
to do just one thing,

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.630
like run an NPM install,
just installing dependencies.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:31.300
And a buildpack group is
a collection of those.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.780
And that lets you reuse
smaller bits of code

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:37.690
like doing a dependency
install, and use

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:39.770
those across different
buildpack groups.

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:41.500
So when most people
say buildpack,

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.333
they're actually talking
about the buildpack

00:07:43.333 --> 00:07:46.280
group that does the full build.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:49.090
But it depends how the
buildpacks are actually

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.490
designed.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:53.610
Finally, there is another
piece of the buildpack

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:54.970
called the stack.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.560
And this is the base
image, or base layer,

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.410
that your application
code is put on top of.

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.630
Here we show an
Ubuntu, stack, which

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:07.290
is what Google Cloud's
buildpacks use.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:10.560
And they can contain
some system libraries

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.330
on top of that base layer.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:15.540
And then your application
code and dependencies

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:20.470
get put at the top
of that layer stack.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:24.370
Buildpacks cannot do an apt-get
to install dependencies.

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:27.340
So those need to be
part of the stack.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.840
And we have designed the
stacks for the Cloud buildpacks

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.440
to contain the most common
things that you likely need.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.020
So you shouldn't have to
redefine that if you're

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.860
using them yourself.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:41.130
So stringing all of that
together, we can see,

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:46.080
here, a flow diagram
where the source code gets

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.010
taken into the builder image.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.463
The buildpacks and
buildpack groups run on it.

00:08:51.463 --> 00:08:53.130
They run the detect
script to figure out

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.540
which language it is.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.670
They run the build script.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:00.830
They install all of your
dependencies, your source code,

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:02.580
whether it's compiled,
or it just, direct,

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.110
goes on top of there.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:06.840
And that gets layered
on top of the stack.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.810
And then that
layer cake that you

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.930
see on the right, the
green and the gray,

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:15.840
that forms the complete
container image, which you

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:18.520
can then run wherever you like.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:22.320
So that's pretty detailed look
into how the buildpack works.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.920
So why would you actually
want to use these things?

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.490
Well, for starters,
a recent study

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:33.300
found that 44% of
containers in the wild

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:37.050
had known vulnerabilities
for which patches existed.

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:40.050
This just shows that
writing your own dockerfile

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:41.910
can actually be quite
fraught with errors

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:43.350
unless you're an
expert, or maybe

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:48.130
have a platform team that
provides dockerfiles for you.

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:52.400
So using buildpacks lets
you circumvent that danger,

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.310
assuming the buildpacks are
designed with security in mind.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:59.460
Now Google Cloud
buildpacks are just that.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:00.710
They've been security audited.

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:04.320
And they're constantly updated,
making sure that they will not

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:05.940
produce vulnerabilities.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.280
Or certainly, at the
bottom layer of the stack,

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:11.460
the base layer doesn't
have any security issues

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:12.750
that we're aware of.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:16.050
The containers are built
according to our container best

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:17.250
practices--

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:18.900
call that the Google way--

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.530
as much as we can
via buildpacks.

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:26.610
And all these things let
you-- like container novices--

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.790
deploy to container
platforms like Kubernetes

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:33.790
without needing to write
their own dockerfiles.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:38.700
And even if your organization
has adopted Docker,

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.370
maybe that adoption
is not even, and there

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.850
are some teams that
haven't yet adopted it,

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:47.160
but they could take advantage
of centralized platforms

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.610
for running their code.

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:52.410
Another use for buildpacks, even
if you do know Docker really

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:55.590
well, is if you just need to
do a quick and easy build.

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:57.480
Maybe you're checking
out an open source

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.600
project or some code
that you don't expect

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.130
to be using long-term,
and you don't

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:04.390
want to write a dockerfile
from scratch for it.

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:09.180
So just use a buildpack, build
it, try it out, and then, boom,

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:10.770
you're done.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:15.450
Some specific Google Cloud
use cases for buildpacks--

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.610
because we're using
buildpacks to power

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:20.040
the builds on App Engine
or Cloud Functions,

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:22.170
if you want to migrate
away from those platforms

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:25.890
to one of our others,
like Cloud Run or GKE,

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:29.460
you could take those App Engine
and Cloud Function buildpacks,

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:32.280
you could continue to run
them against your source code,

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:38.260
and have the same build process
happening on your new platform.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:40.020
And as you know, when
you're migrating,

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:42.420
keeping as many things
constant as possible

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:45.240
is very, very helpful to
minimize the disruption

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.850
from that migration.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:51.120
Finally, another reason
to use buildpacks

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.460
is because they are so
well-integrated into so

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:56.610
many parts of Google
Cloud, like Cloud Build,

00:11:56.610 --> 00:12:02.040
that you can reuse the
knowledge and those tools

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:05.110
across many parts of
your cloud platform.

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:07.530
So now let's take
a look at support

00:12:07.530 --> 00:12:09.660
for buildpacks on Google Cloud.

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:11.820
First of all, we already
went over the open source

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:13.020
buildpacks.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.720
You are free to fork those.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:18.070
Modify them however you'd like.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:19.240
It's quite easy to do.

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:22.200
And in the README,
you'll find documentation

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:23.790
on how to do that.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:27.990
Cloud Build, which is
our builder as a service,

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:30.390
now has native support
for buildpacks.

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:33.780
So if you have source
code and want it built,

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:35.790
instead of running
buildpacks locally,

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:38.430
you can just push
that to Cloud Build,

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:40.290
and it will build it for you.

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:44.310
At the moment, that
support is CLI only.

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:48.850
But support in the console
will be coming soon.

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:53.020
Called Run, our next-generation
serverless platform,

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:56.240
supports direct source
deployments with buildpacks.

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.920
So you can just do a Cloud Run
deploy, put your source code,

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:03.470
and it will build and
deploy that all in one step.

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:06.790
You can also set up
continuous integration

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:08.800
with both Cloud
Build and Cloud Run,

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:12.850
where, every time you update
your source repository,

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:16.300
it will do a fresh build
of that source using

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:20.330
buildplaques-- buildpacks--
and deploy to Cloud Run.

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:22.580
Cloud Shell, which you can
access through the Cloud

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:26.630
Console, already has
the buildpack tool pack

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:27.390
pre-installed.

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:30.710
So you don't need to do that
if you are using our shell.

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:33.980
Like we talked about already,
App Engine and Cloud Functions

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:38.870
both are building with
buildpacks already.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:45.260
And finally, Cloud Code lets
you deploy to Cloud Run or GKE

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:47.300
using buildpacks as
the build mechanism.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:50.420
And it actually does that
via Skaffold, which also now

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:52.700
has native support
for buildpacks--

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:55.880
so a really broad
swath of support

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:57.410
for buildpacks
across Cloud, which

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:59.000
is really pretty exciting.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.180
And now let's take a
look at that in action.

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:06.830
And I will show you a soup to
nuts built in deployment using

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:08.960
buildpacks on Google Cloud.

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:12.260
Let's take a look at how to use
buildpacks with Google Cloud.

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:13.730
So I'm back at my terminal.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:22.010
And I have a pretty
simple Go app right here--

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:24.690
little different from the one
we were looking at before.

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:27.930
And if we just take a look
at the main HTML file,

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:32.270
you can see it's a
pretty boilerplate app.

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:36.110
And all it does once you get
past all that CSS is just

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:39.830
pulls some environment
variables from the request,

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:42.930
and says hello to you,
and displays those.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.390
So let's build it.

00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:47.432
But let's start by
assuming we actually

00:14:47.432 --> 00:14:48.890
haven't installed
the pack command,

00:14:48.890 --> 00:14:51.660
and just do the entire
build in the cloud.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:56.210
So I'm going to use the
gcloud builds submit.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.640
And I'm just going to
give it the pack flag

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:02.570
to tell it to build
this with a buildpack.

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:04.790
And then I'm going to
pass along the name

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:09.830
of the image and the full
Container Registry address

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:10.920
that I want to use.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:14.460
So I'm just going to
call this one run.

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:16.590
So we do that.

00:15:16.590 --> 00:15:19.170
And we can see it is
creating a tar ball

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:25.200
and uploading my app into Google
Cloud, into a storage bucket.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:28.450
And then it is going to
initiate that buildpack.

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:32.370
And you can see, right here,
it is pulling down the builder.

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:35.250
These are the Docker
layers being pulled down.

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:39.345
And it's already cached, so
it should be pretty fast.

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:48.180
And so now it's running
the detection phase

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:48.990
of the buildpack.

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:50.823
That's where it decides
what language it is.

00:15:50.823 --> 00:15:53.730
And it should show us
that-- yep-- it is using Go.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:55.320
And then it does the build.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:16:00.450
It's going to compile my
Go code using Go 1.14.

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:03.030
And it is doing that now.

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:05.220
Looks like it just finished.

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:10.560
And now it's going to create
my final Docker image,

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:15.310
putting my application
code on top of the stack.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:16.110
So it's doing that.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:19.230
And it looks like it is now
putting the completed image

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:20.640
into my Container Registry.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:22.570
And that is all done.

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:26.880
So now that we have a
built container image,

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:29.050
let's deploy it.

00:16:29.050 --> 00:16:31.800
So to deploy it, I'm
going to use Cloud Run.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:37.290
That is Google's next-generation
serverless container engine.

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:42.720
And I'm just going to tell it
to deploy a new service to Cloud

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:46.140
Run, and using that
image that we just

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:48.970
created from Cloud Build.

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:49.790
So I fire this up.

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:51.498
And it's going to ask
me a few questions.

00:16:51.498 --> 00:16:55.340
I'm going to choose our fully
managed Cloud Run platform.

00:16:55.340 --> 00:17:00.170
You can, of course, also
use Cloud Run for Anthos

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:04.460
if you have installed that on
top of your Anthos cluster.

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:06.170
I haven't, so I'm
just going to choose

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:08.540
the fully managed option.

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:16.960
And I'm going to call this
my demo-run-for-screencast

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:18.609
service.

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:19.984
That should be unique.

00:17:19.984 --> 00:17:21.109
And I'll choose the region.

00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:23.150
Let's go US Central.

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:26.800
And allow unauthenticated-- just
to make this more interesting,

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:30.620
let's just make this
a private service

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:33.450
that you need to be
authenticated to use.

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:35.790
So I'm doing that.

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:39.900
And now it is
deploying the service.

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:43.900
And Cloud Run services
had revisions.

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:45.960
This will be the first revision.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:47.700
And now its routing
traffic to it.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:49.230
So we're all done.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:52.470
And it gives me this
URL right here, which I

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:55.380
can use to access the service.

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:59.328
And I am going to hit that up.

00:17:59.328 --> 00:18:01.620
I'm just going to use the
curl command right here so we

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.180
stay right in the terminal.

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:05.790
And here's my command.

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:07.800
I'm curling that same URL.

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:12.480
And you can see I'm adding a
header with an authorization

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:13.210
field.

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:16.260
And then I'm going to call
the gcloud command again

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:19.180
to give it my identity token.

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:20.460
So I do that.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.290
And it's firing it up.

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:25.710
And, boom, we get a
bunch of HTML back

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:27.750
that is that container.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:30.180
You can see, if I
do that same thing

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:34.230
but don't pass the
authorization token,

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:38.730
then I get a forbidden error.

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:41.350
Because I haven't
authenticated properly.

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:47.580
So there you just saw us
building some application code

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:53.190
using our service, Cloud Build,
with its brand-new native

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:54.660
buildpack integration.

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:58.410
And then it stored that
in the Container Registry.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:00.930
And then we created
a brand new Cloud Run

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:06.090
service that was protected
via authentication.

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:08.160
And that all took
just a couple minutes.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.350
And that is one of
the easiest ways

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:14.460
I know to build a new service
and deploy it to the cloud

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:15.690
securely.

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:17.930
Thanks very much.

00:19:17.930 --> 00:19:20.920
So that was buildpacks in
action on Google Cloud.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:25.720
So in this talk, we have
explained what buildpacks are,

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:27.460
why you want to
use them, and how

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:29.050
to use them on Google Cloud.

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:30.970
I hope you enjoyed
it, and I hope

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:33.710
you'll try buildpacks out soon.

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:36.780
Thanks very much,
and have a great day.

